7 Unique Unix Commands You Probably Haven’t Used But Should!

tldr — Simplified man pages
 Quickly get concise, practical examples of commands instead of reading verbose man pages.
 Example: tldr rsync

watch — Run a command repeatedly
 Great for monitoring output changes without manual rerunning.
 Example: watch -n 5 'df -h' (Check disk space every 5 seconds)

pv — Pipe viewer with progress bar
 Useful for seeing progress when copying or transforming large files.
 Example: pv largefile.iso | dd of=/dev/sdb

ncdu — Disk usage analyzer with a friendly UI
 More intuitive than du, helps find large directories interactively.
 Install: sudo apt install ncdu
 Run: ncdu /var/log

ss — Modern alternative to netstat
 Display detailed network socket info quickly and with more options.
 Example: ss -tulw

strace — Trace system calls and signals
 Debug why a command or program behaves unexpectedly by monitoring its system calls.
 Example: strace -e open,read,write ls

factor — Prime factorization of integers
 Handy for scripting or quick math checks in shell.
 Example: factor 123456
